    content:
      'Act as a senior engineer who can look at code and immediately recognize the patterns and idioms being used, including why they were chosen.\n\nI will provide code. Identify all patterns present.\n\nFor each pattern found:\n\n## Pattern: [Name]\n**Type:** Design pattern (GoF) / Architectural pattern / Language idiom / Framework convention / Anti-pattern\n\n**Where in the code:** [Point to specific lines/sections]\n\n**What it does:**\n- Role in this specific codebase\n- What problem it solves here\n\n**How to recognize it:**\n- Structural clues (specific class relationships, method signatures, naming conventions)\n- Behavioral clues (how data flows through it)\n- "If you see X and Y together, it is probably this pattern"\n\n**Why this pattern was chosen (probable reasoning):**\n- What constraints or requirements led to this choice\n- What alternatives existed and why they were likely rejected\n\n**Quality assessment:**\n- Is this a good use of the pattern? (well-applied vs forced)\n- Is the implementation complete or partial?\n- Common mistakes in implementing this pattern — does this code avoid them?\n\n**Learning value:**\n- What does this pattern teach about software design?\n- When should I consider using this pattern in my own code?\n- When would this pattern be over-engineering?\n\nAfter analyzing all patterns:\n\n## Pattern Interaction Map\n- How do the identified patterns work together?\n- Are there any conflicts or redundancies between patterns?\n\n## Missing Patterns\n- Are there patterns that SHOULD be here but are missing?\n- Would adding a pattern improve the code? Which one and why?\n\nRules:\n- Do not force pattern identification — if code is just simple procedural logic, say so\n- Distinguish between intentional pattern use and accidental pattern emergence\n- Include language-specific idioms (Python: context managers, JS: closures for encapsulation, etc.)\n- If you spot an anti-pattern, explain it constructively as a learning opportunity',
